    title: "A job for the smallest fragments",
    text: "A. An archaeologist opening a sample bag may find objects far smaller than the pots and tools usually displayed in museums. In a classroom reconstruction at East Moor, a bag of floor material contained tiny fragments of shell. Students initially proposed that the people who used the room had eaten shellfish. The lecturer asked a simpler question. Had the fragments reached the room while it was occupied, or later, when material was brought in to level the ground? The presence of a fragment was clear. Its route to that position was not.\n\nB. The reconstruction contained three layers. The lowest was a firm occupation surface, the middle a thin covering of ash, and the highest a deposit added well after the building was abandoned. Samples were taken from small squares rather than from an entire layer at once. This preserved information about distribution. Ten fragments from a single square beside a doorway tell a different story from ten fragments scattered evenly across a floor. The total number is identical, but the pattern is not. Each sample bag carried the square identifier outside and a second small label inside. This precaution seemed unnecessary until a damp morning made the writing on the outside of two bags difficult to read. The material inside was still available for sorting, but a count without a location could not contribute to a map of concentrations. With the inner label, the students could restore the missing link. The lecturer used the incident to make a broader point. Looking after a sample includes looking after information that cannot be recovered simply by examining the sample more carefully. A larger microscope would not reveal which square a bag had come from.\n\nC. To recover small pieces, the team washed samples through screens. A coarse screen was quick to use but allowed the smallest pieces to escape. A very fine screen retained them, although it also trapped grains of material that took hours to sort. The students chose two screen sizes and split each sample before washing it. They could then estimate how much the choice of screen affected the result. Washing every early sample through a coarse screen and every late sample through a fine one would have confused a technical difference with a historical change.\n\nD. In the central room, the greatest concentration of shell occurred beside a damaged wall. Students considered a meal eaten there and the loss of material from the wall itself. A block of plaster from the same building contained shell of a similar size. That finding made the second explanation more plausible, especially because several fragments retained white material on one surface. It did not rule out food preparation elsewhere in the room. The team's statement was deliberately limited to the concentration beside the wall.\n\nE. A different puzzle arose in an outdoor square, where the fragments were unusually smooth. A student argued that they had been washed a long distance in a stream. To test an alternative, the group placed fragments in bags with sand and shook them for different periods. Some surfaces became smooth without ever entering flowing water. The exercise showed that more than one process could produce a similar appearance. It gave no direct measure of how far the original material had travelled. The bags differed from a stream in obvious respects. Their sand did not constantly change direction, and the same fragments remained together throughout the exercise. Nobody attempted to set a minute of shaking equal to a fixed distance in running water. Instead, the experiment challenged one necessary step in the original argument. The student had assumed that a smooth surface required transport by a stream. Once a different process produced a similar surface, that requirement no longer held. Evidence of a former water channel close to the square might still strengthen the stream explanation, but it would be additional evidence from the site itself.\n\nF. In the final display each conclusion occupied the same amount of space as the observation on which it depended. A visitor could see photographs of a plaster block, counts from individual squares and examples from the washing experiment. The team did not claim that one small fragment settled the history of the room. Small material became informative when its location, recovery method and plausible alternatives were kept together. A more impressive display might have told a single vivid story, but it would have concealed the choices that made the story possible.",
