    title: "Why an extra clock did not solve the queue",
    text: "A. At the fictional Marlow advice centre, visitors complained about the time between taking a number and meeting an adviser. The manager installed a large clock in the waiting room. The complaints continued. Some visitors even said the queue felt longer. Before treating this as a failure of the clock, an analyst asked what the centre meant by waiting time. Its computer measured the interval from registration at the desk to the moment an adviser opened a case. Visitors often began counting earlier, when they joined the line outside the building.\n\nB. The analyst spent four mornings noting times at the outer door, at the desk and at the advisers' rooms. The longest delay, on average, occurred before registration. At that point the visitors did not yet appear in the computer's list of waiting cases. The manager had previously added an adviser on busy mornings. That could reduce delay once a case was registered but could do little for a slow desk. The centre decided to compare a second registration worker with the extra adviser on different mornings. The registration desk itself performed several activities. A worker checked the visitor's name, found out what kind of help was required and sometimes explained why another organisation might be more suitable. It would have been easy to shorten the desk interview by removing the last activity. That might merely have moved the conversation into the adviser's room, making a later part of the process longer. The analyst therefore followed the same visitor through the building whenever practical. A change that looked successful from a desk worker's viewpoint could be disappointing to somebody interested in the time between entering the building and receiving useful advice.\n\nC. A direct comparison was more difficult than it sounded. Mondays attracted people collecting standard forms, while Thursdays attracted people needing help with unfamiliar letters. The latter took longer to register. Moving a second worker to Thursdays and an adviser to Mondays would not fairly compare the two roles. The centre instead alternated the arrangements on the same weekday over several weeks and recorded how many visitors needed an interpreter. This did not remove all differences, but it addressed two important ones.\n\nD. Averages concealed another issue. Imagine four visitors waiting five minutes each and a fifth waiting forty minutes. Their average is twelve minutes. A notice saying the usual wait is twelve minutes tells none of the five exactly what happened to them. The analyst proposed publishing a range and reporting how often someone waited longer than half an hour. The centre retained the average as well, because managers wanted a simple figure for tracking broad changes. Different measures answered different questions. A range has weaknesses too. One unusual case could make a generally efficient service appear unpredictable if the notice gave only its shortest and longest waits. Staff discussed giving a range that covered most visits and clearly saying what proportion it represented. They also considered whether a visitor needed the figures before travelling to the centre or only after arriving. A person deciding when to leave home could use information about typical mornings. Someone already holding a numbered ticket needed an estimate based on the cases in the room that day. A public notice could provide the former even if a member of staff supplied the latter.\n\nE. A volunteer suggested handing out an estimated service time at the door. Visitors liked the idea when asked in advance. In a small trial, however, a precise promise caused problems when a complicated case delayed everyone behind it. A paper slip saying ten thirty was read as an appointment. When it became clear that advice might start between ten thirty and eleven, visitors could make a more informed choice about leaving to buy a drink. The trial therefore switched from exact times to short intervals, with an explanation of why an interval might move.\n\nF. In the final review, the number of complaints had fallen but had not reached zero. The centre had changed the registration arrangement, the way it explained delays and the type of information shown in the waiting room. Because these changes overlapped, the analyst refused to give all the credit to any one of them. The clock remained on the wall. Its usefulness now depended on the other information beside it. A precise instrument for measuring minutes had never been enough, on its own, to define which minutes mattered.",
